1. @RequestMapping - A general purpose mapping annotation which is used at the class level
                  It avoids repeating the common URL.

Example: Without it
         @GetMapping("/employees")
         @PostMapping("/employees")
         @DeleteMapping("/employees/{id}")

         With it
         @RestController
         @RequestMapping("/employees")
         public class EmployeeController{
            @GetMapping
            @PostMapping
            @DeleteMapping("/{id}")
         }

         Much cleaner code

2. @GetMapping - Used when we want to handle an HTTP GET request

                 @GeMapping - maps an HTTP GET request to a controller method. It is a shortcut for
                 @RequestMapping(method = RequestMethod.GET)

Example:
        @GetMapping("/employees")
        public List<Employee> getEmployees(){
            return employeeService.getAllEmployees();
        }

3. @PostMapping

   Used for creating a resource.

   @PostMapping
   public Employee createEmployee(@RequestBody Employee employee) {
       return employeeService.createEmployee(employee);
   }

   The client might send: JSON

   {
       "name": "Sneha",
       "department": "IT",
       "salary": 70000
   }

   Request:

   POST /employees

4. @RequestBody - tells Spring that "take the data from the HTTP request body and convert into this Java object"

    Example:

        @PostMapping
        public void creatEmployee(@RequestBody Employee employee){
            employeeService.creatEmployee(employee);
        }

        Client sends: JSON

        {
            "name": "Sneha",
            "department": "IT"
        }

        Spring converts it roughly like:

        JSON
         | to
        Employee object

        So inside your method:
        employee.getName()
        gives:
        Sneha

Note: RequestBody = data coming inside the request body.

      Usually used with:
        POST
        PUT
        PATCH

5. @PathVariable - Used to bind values from HTTP request URL to the Argument in the method signature

Example:

    URL: /employees/101

    @GetMapping("/{id}")
    public Employee getEmployeeWithId(@PathVariable Long id){
        return employeeService.getEmployeeWithId(id);
    }

    Here Spring extracts id - 101 and gives it to Long id. So now id = 101

6. @RequestParam - extracts a query parameter, such as /employees?department=IT

Example:  GET /employees?department=IT
          here
          department=IT is the query parameter

          @GetMapping
          public List<Employee> getEmployeesByDept(@RequestParam String department){
            return employeeService.getAllEmployeesWithDept(department);
          }

          Request: GET /employees?department=IT

          Spring gives: department="IT"

Difference between the @PathVariable and @RequestMapping
---------------------------------------------------------

    @PathVariable

    Value is part of the URL path:

    /employees/101
    @PathVariable Long id

    Used when identifying a specific resource.

    @RequestParam

    Value comes after ?

    /employees?department=IT
    @RequestParam String department

    Used commonly for filtering, searching, sorting, pagination, etc.

7. @PutMapping - Used for Updating a Resource

Example:
        Request - PUT /employees/101

        JSON - {
                    "name": "Sneha",
                    "dept": "ME",
                    "salary": 80000
                }

          @PutMapping
          public Employee updateEmployeeById(@PathVariable Long id, @RequestBody Employee employee){
                return employeeService.updateEmployeeById(id,employee);
          }

We are using two annotations together here:
          @PathVariable
          @RequestBody

          Because: 101 -> @PathVariable and Employee JSON -> @RequestBody

8. @DeleteMapping - Used to delete a resource

Example: Request: DELETE /employees/101

        @DeleteMapping
        public void deleteEmpById(@PathVariable Long id){
            employeeService.deleteEmpById(id);
        }

        Spring extracts: 101
        using @PathVariable

One important thing about @RequestMapping
-------------------------------------------

We can actually use @RequestMapping instead of a specialized annotations

For example:

@RequestMapping(
    value="{/id}",
    method=RequestMethod.GET
)
public void getAllEmployeeById(@PathVariable int id){
...
}

This is equivalent to:

@GetMapping("/{id}")
public void getEmpById(@PathVariable int id){
...
}

Similarly,

@RequestMapping(
    value = {"/id"}
    method = RequestMethod.PUT
)
public void updateEmpById(@PathVariable int id, @RequestBody Employee employee){
...
}

This is equivalent to:

@PutMapping("/{id}")
public void updateEmployeeById(@PathVariable int id, @RequestBody Employee employee){
...
}

So:

@GetMapping, @PostMapping, @PutMapping, and @DeleteMapping are specialized shortcuts for @RequestMapping.

Important Notes:
------------------

- @RequestMapping is used to map HTTP requests to controller classes or methods. It can specify the URL path,
  HTTP method, headers, parameters, and other request conditions.

- @RequestMapping is a general-purpose mapping annotation that can handle different HTTP methods, whereas
  @GetMapping specifically maps GET requests. @GetMapping is a composed shortcut for
  @RequestMapping(method = RequestMethod.GET)

- @PathVariable extracts a value from the URL path, such as /employees/101,
  whereas @RequestParam extracts a query parameter, such as /employees/search?department=IT

  @PathVariable is used when we want to identify specific data
  @RequestParam is used in operations like searching, sorting, filtering, pagination etc.

- @RequestBody tells Spring to read the HTTP request body and deserialize it into a Java object,
  typically from JSON

- We can use @PathVariable and @RequestBody together. Example: Update emp with id 101, JSON passed from body

